# Fluorine‐Boosted Kinetic and Selective Molecular Sieving of C6 Derivatives

## abstract
Porous molecular sorbents have excellent selectivity towards hydrocarbon separation with energy saving techniques. However, to realize commercialization, molecular sieving processes should be faster and more efficient compared to extended frameworks. In this work, we show that utilizing fluorine to improve the hydrophobic profile of leaning pillararenes affords a substantial kinetic selective adsorption of benzene over cyclohexane (20 : 1 for benzene). The crystal structure shows a porous macrocycle that acts as a perfect match for benzene in both the intrinsic and extrinsic cavities with strong interactions in the solid state. The fluorinated leaning pillararene surpasses all reported organic molecular sieves and is comparable to the extended metal-organic frameworks that were previously employed for this separation such as UIO-66. Most importantly, this sieving system outperformed the well-known zeolitic imidazolate frameworks under low pressure, which opens the door to new generations of molecular sieves that can compete with extended frameworks for more sustainable hydrocarbon separation.

One of the most difficult industrial challenges to achieve is identifying promising methods for benzene/cyclohexane separation.[1–4] Cyclohexane is synthesized through catalytic hydrogenation of benzene, with unreacted benzene removed to achieve high cyclohexane purity.[5–6] The importance of cyclohexane stems largely from its conversion to the intermediate cyclohexanone, which functions as a feedstock for Nylon precursors such as adipic acid, caprolactam, and hexamethylenediamine.[7] Traditional methods of separation, such as distillation, are unable to separate benzene and cyclohexane due to their comparable physical properties.[8] Extractive and azeotropic distillations are the most used industrial methods for separating such mixtures. Nonetheless, these processes necessitate complex operation and high energy consumption, resulting in high operating costs. In terms of adsorptive separation, polymers[9] and metal– organic framework (MOFs)[10–11] are the most commonly used materials. Nonporous adaptive crystals (NACs)[12] and most recently metallacycles[13] have also been employed for this type of separation. However, very slow adsorption kinetics throughout the separation process was the main barrier to using molecular-based sieving platforms for this type of separation when compared to extended frameworks.[14–15] This was clearly seen when NACs and other nonporous organic cages were used for hydrocarbon separation as in spite of the high selectivity, the separation process was too slow compared to hybrid and inorganic adsorbents.[16–18]
Pillararenes are a relatively new class of organic macrocycles that have received much attention since their discovery by Ogoshi and co-workers.[19–22] Different subclasses of pillararenes were synthesized and studied starting with the inaugural work of Yang and Sue.[19,23–27] This was followed by multiple attempts to use the intrinsic pore of pillararene and its derivatives for various separations, such as gas,[28–29] vapor,[30] or liquid[31–34] through host -guest interactions.[35–36] However, the main dilemma of slow kinetics hampered major translational efforts using this platform.
From a design perspective, improving hydrophobicity of the sieving platform can improve the kinetics of hydrocarbon separation. This has been verified in organic cages and MOFs where using fluorine improved not only hydrophobicity but also thermal stability.[37–45] Herein, an adaptive porous fluorinated leaning pillararene was synthesized with persistent porosity and was efficiently employed to separate benzene traces after catalytic hydrogenation to produce high purity cyclohexane under dynamic conditions (Figure 1). This fluorinated molecular sieving platform showed a relatively fast adsorption, which was comparable to MOFs with high selectivity and reproducibility. We envisage that this work will propel more work on designing hydrophobic molecular sieves for energy-efficient hydrocarbon separation.
The fluorinated leaning pillararenes were synthesized via a simple two-step synthesis (Scheme S1). The use of hexafluoroisopropanol (HFIP), which has recently attracted a lot of attention for its ability to enable unique modes of reactivity was critical to the success of this pathway.[46] Despite the fact that the (perfluoro-1,4- phenylene)dimethanol ring is highly electron poor, due to the four fluorine deactivating groups, the fluorinated ligand can still be synthesized via a Friedel–Crafts reaction aided by HFIP and TfOH at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .[46] Following the synthesis of the fluorinated monomer (1), the leaning pillararene(2) was successfully synthesized following the classical conditions (Scheme S1).[47] $\mathrm{^1H}$ , $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR, and HRMS collectively confirm that the fluorinated monomer is produced (Figure S1a, S2–S3). The NMR spectra match our target structure, and the major $[\mathbf{M}+\mathbf{Na}]^{+}$ peak at $473.14\mathrm{m/z}$ corresponds to the ligand (1). $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ , $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ , $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ NMR, DEPT-45, DEPT-90, DEPT-135, COSY NMR, and HRMS all confirmed the formation of the fluorinated leaning pillararene(2)(Figure S1b, S4–S10). The HRMS revealed a distinctive $[\mathbf{M}+\mathbf{Na}]^{+}$ peak at $947.29\mathrm{m/z}$ , corresponding to the molecular weight of the fluorinated leaning pillararene (Figure S1b). We then studied the effect of the fluorination on the reactivity of the leaning pillararene. We successfully demethylate the macrocycle with the regular demethylating agent BBr3 to obtain perhydroxylated pillaraene, which was then characterized using various techniques (Figure S11a, S12–S13) .We were also able to obtain the quinone derivatives of the macrocycle using CAN, which was then thoroughly characterized (Figure S11b, S14–S15). We concluded that the presence of perfluorinated phenyl rings has no effect on the reactivity to demethylation or oxidation while maintaining the ring‘s integrity.
A crystal structure for the macrocycle (2) was also obtained, providing insight into the cavity shape and system packing. The two opposite dimethoxy benzene rings are parallel, which differs from the previously reported leaning pillararene structure (Figure 2, S16–S20). The “tilting” in our structure comes from fluorinated benzenes, which alters the structure‘s packing. We can see an interaction between methoxy CH and fluorine in the packing structure between the pillararene rings (Figure S21–S22, Table S2, S3). To recrystallize the macrocycles, various solvents were tested. We were able to grow a high-quality crystal using the solvents DCM/Hexane. The inclusion of hexane in the macrocycles (Figure S20)[48] inspired us to use it as an adsorbent after activation keeping the channels accessible for vapor adsorption. We measured the surface area of the activated crystalline material of (2) and a BET surface area of around $200\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Figure 3a) was recorded (crystallization using DCM/ Hexane). Using NMR spectroscopy after incubation with different hydrocarbon vapors, this activated macrocycle demonstrated a very high affinity for benzene $\approx85\%$ selectivity (Figure S23).
We then used benzene for crystallization to obtain $\pmb{2}\textcircled{a}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{Z}$ crystals (Figure 2). The single crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) analysis showed that $\pmb{2}@\mathbf{B}\mathbf{z}$ crystallizes in a triclinic system with a P-1 space group (Table S1). The asymmetric unit consists of two halves of (2) host molecules with two halves of benzene guest molecules in its intrinsic cavity and other three benzene molecules stacked within the channel of the crystal packing (Figure 2). The structure analysis reveals that the benzene guest molecules appear almost planar in the intrinsic cavity of (2) and are stabilized exclusively by six web-like host–guest $\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}{\cdots}{\pi}$ intermolecular interactions (Figure 2, S24 and Table S4) while the other benzene molecules within the channels of the crystal packing are stabilized by some other hydrogen bonding intermolecular interactions such as C H···O, C H···F and $\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{H}{\cdots}{\pi}$ intermolecular interactions (Figure S22 and Table S5). Furthermore, each molecule of (2) is connected through strong host-host $\mathrm{C-H\mathrm{\cdot\cdot}O}$ and $\mathrm{C-H\cdotsF}$ (Figure S22 and Table S3). Interestingly, the crystal packing shows that molecules of (2) stack in window-to-window form creating 1D channel occupied by benzene guest molecules when viewed down aaxis (Figure S25). The overall crystal packing arrangements of (2) with benzene guest molecules along all the crystallographic axes confirm its host/guest behavior/performance (Figure 2). We realized that the interaction was very strong, implying that this macrocycle could be a good candidate for removing/separating benzene. Then we decided to measure the static solvent/vapor isotherm for benzene and cyclohexane, to see if we could use this macrocycle for their separation. The crystalline $\pmb{2}@\mathrm{Hex}$ was activated at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without vacuum (Figure S26) and we were able to get a good quality crystal that diffracts. The single crystal analysis revealed open channels, which explains the porosity. The activated material was tested for benzene/cyclohexane vapor adsorption where it shows high benzene uptake compared to cyclohexane. Surprisingly, the benzene isotherm demonstrated a breathing behavior at about 0.8 relative pressure, where the amount of benzene adsorbed was doubled (Figure 3b). We hypothesized that the amount was doubled because the macrocycle has a tendency to hold more benzene molecules in its both extrinsic/intrinsic cavities as proved earlier. However, we did not observe this type of behavior in the cyclohexane isotherm (Figure 3c). This behavior assisted us in demonstrating that the macrocycle is only adaptive and selective for benzene. The pore size distribution was also calculated as shown in Figure 3d. When we measured the kinetic uptake of both vapors, we realized that the macrocycle can uptake benzene much faster than cyclohexane in a very short time (Figure S27). Looking back on the adsorption behavior of molecular sieving platforms, it was clear that in all of the types reported to date, few hours are required to reach saturation, whereas in our case, the adsorption kinetics were extremely fast measuring $60\mathrm{{s}}$ for saturation (Figure S27).
A simple comparison of macrocycle (2) to different types of adsorbents (organic and hybrid) reveals that our macrocycles outperform the organic adsorbents in terms of benzene uptake at high and low pressure. When compared to ZIF-8 for instance, this macrocycle has lower benzene uptake at $({\mathrm{P}}/{\mathrm{P}}0){\approx}1$ but higher uptake at low pressure $(\mathrm{P/}$ ${\mathrm{P0}})<0.1$ , which is highly applicable to real industrial separation[14–15]( Figure 4).
The $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{6}/C_{6}H_{12}}$ separation performance of compound (2) under dynamic conditions was investigated in a breakthrough setup using a $1/1$ $\left(\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}\right)$ mixture of $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{12}$ at $303\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 5). In the breakthrough curve of (2), $\mathrm{{C}_{6}\mathrm{{H}_{12}}}$ was eluted before $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{6}}$ (Figure 5). This result indicates that (2) preferentially adsorbs $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{6}}$ over $\mathrm{{C}_{6}\mathrm{{H}_{12}}}$ . It’s worth to mention that we noticed a roll-up effect on cyclohexane with increasing the amount of adsorbed benzene in the vapor which also proves the responsive behavior in the presence of benzene for the macrocycle (2). The benzene uptake was calculated to be $0.42\mathrm{mmol/g}$ and the selectivity of benzene separation to be 20: 1.
DFT calculations were also employed to gain a thorough understanding of benzene‘s selectivity over cyclohexane. The binding energy of $2@\mathbf{B}\mathbf{z}$ (B1) is determined to be $-2.31\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ (Figure 6, S28), indicating that benzene encapsulation in the cavity is preferred and the host–guest complex is more stable. The binding energy of $2\textcircled{a}$ Cyclohexane (FC) is $1.77\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ , indicating that cyclohexane capture in the macrocycle is unfavorable (Figure 6, S28). As a result, the macrocycle (2) exhibited high benzene selectivity over cyclohexane. Moreover, the binding energy value of $2@\mathbf{B}\mathbf{z}$ is indicating the favorable host–guest interaction than that of a non-fluorinated macrocycle leaning pillararene (NFB) with benzene $\left(-1.21\mathrm{kcal/mol}\right)$ . As a result, there is no doubt that the addition of fluorine to the macrocycle significantly helps in the formation of a more stable host–guest combination with benzene.
The study of the estimated Hirshfeld surfaces and the resulting fingerprint plots may offer a qualitative and quantitative description of the involvement of supramolecular interactions within the host–guest crystal system (Figure S29). Hirshfeld surface and fingerprint plots were used to study and compare the elaborate interactions of several guests, cyclohexane and benzenes (B1 B5, NFB), as shown (Figure S29–31). It is found that the $\mathrm{^{C-H}}$ interaction dominates the intermolecular interactions for the benzenes in the intrinsic pore of (2) (B1 and B2), resulting in a significant binding affinity. The strong $\mathrm{C-H}$ contact is mostly due to interactions between the six host benzene rings and the enclosed guest hydrogens (Figure S30). C HF interactions (12 percent 22 percent) play a key function in the benzenes inside the channels of 2 (B3␀B5), making up for the loss in $\mathrm{C-H}$ interactions. This conclusion is consistent with their binding energy analyses, which is responsible for the benzene selectivity. In this sense, the medium $\mathrm{C-H}$ interaction $(40\%)$ of NFB without C-HF contact is insufficient to achieve strong binding. As a result, the addition of fluorine improves the binding capacity of leaning pillararene to achieve benzene selectivity.
In summary, we were able to demonstrate that fast adsorption kinetics can be attained by employing porous molecular sieving platforms. Fluorinated leaning pillararenes exhibited 20 :1 benzene selectivity over cyclohexane and could be easily regenerated and used in multiple cycles with no loss of performance. Most importantly, breakthrough and kinetics measurements showed an unprecedented fast adsorption profile where saturation in the case of benzene could be reached in $60{\mathrm{~s}}$ . This work highlights the importance of improving hydrophobicity in molecular adsorbents, for non-covalent interactions in guest recognition, to promote dynamic and translational processes for hydrocarbon separation.
